<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §01 ═══ -->
<section id="s-dilemma" data-code="dilemma">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Memory Dilemma</div>
<h2>Two bad choices: forget or pay O(L²)</h2>
<p>
  Recurrent neural networks — Linear Attention, Mamba, Titans — compress the entire past into a
  fixed-size hidden state <em>M</em>. That state never grows: processing token 4,096 takes the same
  memory as token 1. The upside is constant-time inference; the downside is that the model must
  continuously <em>overwrite</em> old information with new. By the time you query about something
  from 2,000 tokens ago, it may have been squeezed out.
</p>
<p>
  Transformers solve this with attention: every past (key, value) pair is cached in the KV store and
  directly accessible. Recall is perfect in principle. The cost is O(L²) compute and O(L) KV cache
  memory that grows without bound.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">memory_dilemma.py — fixed vs growing memory</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a token to query it</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="dilemma-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button id="dilemma-rnn" class="step-nav button" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s">RNN (forget)</button>
      <button id="dilemma-mc" class="step-nav button" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s">MC (cache)</button>
      <button id="dilemma-tf" class="step-nav button" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s">Transformer (O(L²))</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The canvas above shows a 16-token sequence. In <strong>RNN mode</strong>, one small box is the
  entire memory — as new tokens arrive, old information fades (α decay). A query to an early token
  returns a faded, approximate answer. In <strong>MC mode</strong>, four segment checkpoints are
  preserved and the query finds the right one. In <strong>Transformer mode</strong>, every past
  token is cached — perfect recall but the cache grows with each step.
</p>

<h3>The formal gap</h3>
<p>
  Linear attention (LA) distills everything into a single matrix <em>M</em>:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Linear Attention Recurrence</div>
  <span class="math-var">M</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">M</span><sub>t−1</sub> + <span class="math-var">v</span><sub>t</sub> φ(<span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub>)<sup>⊤</sup><br>
  <span class="math-var">y</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">M</span><sub>t</sub> φ(<span class="math-var">q</span><sub>t</sub>)
  <span class="math-comment" style="margin-left:1rem">— one d×d matrix for the entire past</span>
</div>
<p>
  The single matrix must encode <em>all</em> key–value associations. For long sequences this
  leads to "memory overflow" — the same cells must store unrelated facts and one overwrites another.
  The fix is obvious in hindsight: don't throw away intermediate states — cache them.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §02 ═══ -->
<section id="s-mc" data-code="mc">
<div class="section-no">02 — Memory Caching</div>
<h2>Segment, compress, cache — then attend to all checkpoints</h2>
<p>
  Memory Caching (MC) adds one idea to any recurrent model: split the input sequence into segments,
  run the recurrence normally within each segment, but <em>save the final hidden state</em> of
  each segment before starting the next one. Each saved state is a <strong>compressed summary</strong>
  of that segment's tokens. When computing the output for a query at time <em>t</em>, the model
  attends to its current (online) memory <em>and</em> all cached past memories:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MC Output Rule</div>
  <span class="math-var">y</span><sub>t</sub> = Agg&nbsp;(
    <span class="math-var">M</span><sup>(1)</sup><sub>end</sub>(q<sub>t</sub>),
    …,
    <span class="math-var">M</span><sup>(s−1)</sup><sub>end</sub>(q<sub>t</sub>);
    &nbsp;<span class="math-var">M</span><sup>(s)</sup><sub>t</sub>(q<sub>t</sub>)&nbsp;)
  <span class="math-comment" style="margin-left:.5rem">— online + N cached states</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">mc_mechanism.py — segmented sequence with cached checkpoints</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">adjust segments N below</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="mc-canvas" style="width:100%;height:230px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.875rem;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <span style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Segments N =</span>
      <button class="mc-seg-btn active" data-n="1">1 (pure RNN)</button>
      <button class="mc-seg-btn" data-n="2">2</button>
      <button class="mc-seg-btn" data-n="4">4</button>
      <button class="mc-seg-btn" data-n="8">8 (near Attn)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
.mc-seg-btn{padding:.28rem .7rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s}
.mc-seg-btn:hover{border-color:var(--cyan);color:var(--cyan)}
.mc-seg-btn.active{border-color:var(--purple);color:var(--purple-l);background:var(--purple-dim)}
</style>

<p>
  The animation shows a 32-token sequence. The tokens are split into <em>N</em> equal segments.
  A <strong>purple diamond</strong> marks each cached memory checkpoint. When the rightmost (current)
  token queries the model, arrows show it attending to every checkpoint plus its own online memory.
  With N=1 there is only online memory — pure RNN. With N=8 almost every 4-token block is cached —
  close to attention.
</p>

<h3>Checkpoint vs. independent memory</h3>
<p>
  There are two ways to initialise segment memories: either continue from the previous segment's
  final state (<strong>checkpoint mode</strong> — like resuming an optimisation run) or start from
  scratch (<strong>independent mode</strong> — each memory compresses only its own tokens, no
  leakage). The paper finds both useful: checkpoint mode gives cheaper computation (no separate
  initialisation), independent mode avoids segment interference for certain architectures.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §03 ═══ -->
<section id="s-variants" data-code="variants">
<div class="section-no">03 — Four Variants</div>
<h2>How to aggregate cached memories</h2>
<p>
  Once you have the cached states, the question is how to combine them with the current online memory.
  The paper proposes four strategies, trading off simplicity, expressivity, and efficiency.
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="variant-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Residual Memory (RM)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Sum all cached memories plus the online memory. Every past segment contributes equally — no learned weights. When the memory module is strictly linear (a matrix), the cached states can be pre-summed, so this collapses to a single larger matrix — equivalent to a standard fixed memory, but bigger.</div>
      <div class="step-code">y_t = M_online(q) + Σ M_cached_i(q)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Gated Residual Memory (GRM) ★ Best overall</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Assign each cached memory an input-dependent gate γ. The gate computes the cosine similarity between the current query and the mean-pooled keys of that segment — so semantically relevant past segments get more weight. This breaks the linear pre-sum collapsing and gives context-aware retrieval.</div>
      <div class="step-code">γ_i = softmax(&#x27E8;u_t, MeanPool(S_i)&#x27E9;)   y_t = Σ γ_i · M_i(q)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Memory Soup</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Rather than summing outputs, interpolate the weight parameters themselves: build a single fused memory module M* whose weights are a convex combination of all cached modules' weights. For non-linear deep memories (like Titans MLP), this is strictly more expressive than GRM — the fusion happens in weight space, not output space.</div>
      <div class="step-code">θ_M* = Σ γ_i · θ_M_i   →   y_t = M*(q)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sparse Selective Caching (SSC) ★ Best efficiency</div>
      <div class="step-desc">MoE-style router selects only the top-k most relevant segments per token. Each token only loads k memory checkpoints instead of all N. The segment mean-pool embeddings can be pre-computed, so routing is parallelisable. Result: near-GRM quality, but with a hardware footprint close to the base RNN.</div>
      <div class="step-code">R_t = TopK(&#123;&#x27E8;u_t, MeanPool(S_i)&#x27E9;&#125;)   y_t = Σ_&#123;i∈R_t&#125; γ_i · M_i(q)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="var-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="var-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="var-next">Next →</button>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §04 ═══ -->
<section id="s-spectrum" data-code="spectrum">
<div class="section-no">04 — Complexity Spectrum</div>
<h2>A dial between O(L) and O(L²)</h2>
<p>
  The number of cached segments N controls where on the spectrum from pure RNN to full Transformer
  you sit. With constant segment size C = L/N, the total compute is <strong>O(L² / C)</strong>.
  Larger segments mean fewer checkpoints (cheaper, but coarser memory); smaller segments mean more
  checkpoints (more recall, closer to attention cost).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">complexity_spectrum.py — memory capacity vs cost</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">bars normalised to Transformer = 100%</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:1.25rem 1.25rem .75rem">
    <div class="bars-section-label">Recall capacity →</div>
    <div id="spectrum-bars"></div>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      N=1 → O(L) &nbsp;|&nbsp; N=C=L/4 → O(L²/4) &nbsp;|&nbsp; N=L → O(L²) Transformer
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Logarithmic segmentation (inspired by Fenwick trees) brings the cost down to <strong>O(L log L)</strong>
  at the price of very coarse early-segment representation — the first half of a 16K sequence is
  compressed into a single giant segment, limiting recall resolution. The paper's experiments show
  constant-size segments consistently outperform logarithmic ones on recall-intensive tasks,
  confirming that resolution matters more than raw complexity reduction.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Complexity Summary</div>
  Pure RNN (N=1)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;: <span class="math-var">O(L)</span><br>
  MC, segment size C&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;: <span class="math-var">O(L² / C)</span><br>
  Log segmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;: <span class="math-var">O(L log L)</span><br>
  Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;: <span class="math-var">O(L²)</span>
</div>

<p>
  An elegant theoretical connection: if you set segment size to 1 (each token is its own segment)
  and use a linear memory module, MC with GRM recovers <em>gated global softmax attention</em>.
  The KV cache is exactly the set of cached memory checkpoints. MC is thus a strict generalisation
  of both pure RNNs and Transformers — with N as the interpolating knob.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §05 ═══ -->
<section id="s-niah" data-code="niah">
<div class="section-no">05 — Long-Context Recall</div>
<h2>Needle-in-a-Haystack: from 44% to 82%</h2>
<p>
  NIAH (Needle-in-a-Haystack) is the canonical stress test for long-context recall. Three difficulty
  levels: <strong>S-NIAH-1</strong> (passkey — easy), <strong>S-NIAH-2</strong> (number in
  haystack — medium), <strong>S-NIAH-3</strong> (UUID — hard).
</p>
<p>
  The baseline DLA model at 16K context achieves only 44% on passkey retrieval — the simplest task.
  DLA + GRM hits <strong>82.4%</strong>. Titans + GRM reaches <strong>100%</strong> across all
  context lengths. The improvement comes directly from cached memories preserving access to the
  distant past that the base model's fixed state had lost.
</p>

