<p>
  One of the starkest results is UUID retrieval (S-NIAH-3) at 8K context: the base Titans scores
  only 42.8%, while Titans + GRM reaches 69%. This task requires matching a long random string —
  essentially no compression is possible — making a fixed-size memory almost useless. Cached
  checkpoints give the model direct access to the token level through the segment summary, closing
  most of the gap.
</p>

<h3>Log-Linear++ comparison</h3>
<p>
  Log-Linear++ uses the same idea (growing set of hidden states via Fenwick tree structure) but
  forces very long initial segments. At 16K context the first segment alone covers 8,192 tokens.
  A single compressed memory for that segment loses resolution. MC with constant segment size of
  256 distributes the compression load more evenly, consistently outperforming Log-Linear++ on
  every retrieval task.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ §06 ═══ -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Downstream Results</div>
<h2>Language modeling and commonsense reasoning</h2>
<p>
  Beyond NIAH, MC improves perplexity and downstream task accuracy consistently. The 1.3B parameter
  Titans + GRM model trained on 100B tokens achieves <strong>Wiki perplexity 15.37</strong>,
  compared to 20.04 for the base Titans and 24.18 for a vanilla Transformer++.
</p>

